---
/* Starlight's page footer: the edit link, the last-updated date and the
 * pagination that sit at the end of an article.
 *
 * The site's copyright is not here. It is chrome rather than the end of a page,
 * so it lives in SiteFooter.astro, which PageFrame renders below the content
 * column entirely.
 *
 * Overriding the component means restating the layout it came with: a
 * component override replaces Starlight's scoped styles along with its markup,
 * so the footer and meta rules below are its own, kept as they were. The
 * `credits` kudos link is not carried over — the site does not enable it, and
 * reproducing it would mean importing an icon from inside Starlight's internals
 * to render something that has never appeared.
 *
 * The three parts are imported from `@astrojs/starlight/components/*`, the
 * published path, rather than the `virtual:starlight/components/*` specifiers
 * Starlight's own footer uses: those are typed in a declaration file the site's
 * tsconfig does not see, and `astro check` fails on all three. The difference
 * that buys Starlight is indirection — a `virtual:` import resolves through any
 * override configured for that component. Nothing here overrides the edit link,
 * the last-updated date or the pagination, so this renders the same thing; a
 * future override of one of them would need this file changed to match.
 */
import EditLink from "@astrojs/starlight/components/EditLink.astro";
import LastUpdated from "@astrojs/starlight/components/LastUpdated.astro";
import Pagination from "@astrojs/starlight/components/Pagination.astro";

/* The edit link and the last-updated date are documentation affordances, and
 * the landing page is not documentation. "Edit page" invites a contributor to
 * change a marketing page, and a date only ever works against a front page: a
 * quiet month reads as an abandoned project to the one visitor least equipped
 * to judge that. Both stay on every other page, where "is this current?" is a
 * question a reader genuinely has.
 *
 * Keyed on the splash template rather than the page id, so a second splash page
 * inherits the same treatment. Pagination already renders nothing here — the
 * landing page is in no sidebar group, so it has no neighbours.
 *
 * The copyright is no longer part of this component. It is chrome, not the end
 * of an article, so it lives in SiteFooter.astro, which PageFrame puts below
 * the content column entirely.
 */
const isSplash = Astro.locals.starlightRoute.entry.data.template === "splash";
---

<footer class="sl-flex">
  {
    !isSplash && (
      <div class="meta sl-flex">
        <EditLink />
        <LastUpdated />
      </div>
    )
  }
  <Pagination />
</footer>

<style>
  footer {
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }

  .meta {
    gap: 0.75rem 3rem;
    justify-content: space-between;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-top: 3rem;
    font-size: var(--sl-text-sm);
    color: var(--sl-color-gray-3);
  }

  .meta > :global(p:only-child) {
    margin-inline-start: auto;
  }

</style>
